      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): ACP session startup fails when backend does not advertise thinking config",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes #103802 — Every `sessions_spawn(runtime: \"acp\")` against backends like OpenCode (which only advertise `mode, model` as supported config options) fails immediately with:\n\n```\nAcpRuntimeError: ACP backend \"acpx\" does not accept config key \"thinking\".: code=ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL\n```\n\nThe ACP runtime resolves a thinking default (e.g., `\"off\"` for non-reasoning provider models) and injects it into spawn options. The backend rejects it because it doesn't advertise thinking as a supported config option. The caller cannot avoid this — passing no `thinking` in `sessions_spawn` does not prevent the injection.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe root cause is in `resolveAcpSpawnRuntimeOptions` (`src/agents/acp-spawn.ts`): when no explicit thinking override is provided, the function falls through to `resolveThinkingDefault` and injects the auto-derived value into spawn options. This value then gets persisted and sent to the backend via `setConfigOption`, which rejects it.\n\nThe fix removes the auto-derivation fallback. If the caller doesn't provide an explicit thinking value, no thinking option is included in spawn options. This means:\n\n- **Auto-derived defaults are never sent** — backends that don't advertise thinking work fine\n- **Explicit user intent still fails visibly** — if someone passes `sessions_spawn(thinking: \"high\")` and the backend doesn't support it, the existing strict advertised-keys check in `applyManagerRuntimeControls` still throws `ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL`, same as before\n- **No new code paths** — just removes the auto-injection, no prevention layer or safety net needed\n\n## User Impact\n\nACP spawns targeting backends with limited config support (mode+model only) succeed instead of failing on init. Backends that support thinking/effort continue to work when explicitly requested. No behavior change for explicit thinking requests on non-supporting backends — they still fail visibly.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 1 file changed, 2 insertions, 15 deletions\n- Removes the auto-derivation block and the now-unused `resolveThinkingDefault` import\n- All existing tests pass, `oxlint` reports 0 errors, 0 warnings\n\n**Terminal evidence (commit d7bccfa):**\n\n```\n$ pnpm exec oxlint src/agents/acp-spawn.ts\nFound 0 warnings and 0 errors.\n```\n\n**Live validation:** The same logic (stop injecting auto-derived thinking) was applied as a compiled JS patch to a running OpenClaw instance targeting an OpenCode ACP backend. Before the patch, every ACP spawn failed with `ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL`. After the patch, the thinking config option was never sent and session startup completed normally through a full first turn.",
        "branch": "fix/acp-opencode-thinking-config",
